WEBVTT

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.425
Indicators of compromise, or IOCs,

00:00:02.425 --> 00:00:06.600
are observable details that may be associated with suspicious or malicious activity.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:11.600
Common examples include IP addresses, domains, URLs, file hashes, email artifacts,

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.505
registry paths, process names, certificate details, and malware configuration values.

00:00:16.505 --> 00:00:20.000
Indicators are useful because they can enrich alerts, support searches,

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.605
help scope incidents, and sometimes drive temporary blocking.

00:00:22.605 --> 00:00:26.085
They are often the first CTI artifacts that new analysts encounter.

00:00:26.085 --> 00:00:27.505
Indicators also have limits.

00:00:27.505 --> 00:00:31.550
Many are brittle, meaning they can change quickly or appear in benign contexts.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:33.495
An IP address can be shared infrastructure.

00:00:33.495 --> 00:00:35.417
A domain can expire or be sinkholed.

00:00:35.417 --> 00:00:38.485
A file hash changes when the file changes by even one byte.

00:00:38.485 --> 00:00:41.000
A process name can be copied from legitimate software.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.105
A registry path may be meaningful in one intrusion but irrelevant in another.

00:00:45.105 --> 00:00:48.638
Using indicators without context can create false positives, missed detections,

00:00:48.638 --> 00:00:50.085
and unnecessary blocks.

00:00:50.085 --> 00:00:53.060
Tactics, techniques, and procedures, usually called TTPs,

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:54.955
describe behavior at a higher level.

00:00:54.955 --> 00:00:59.000
A tactic is the adversary's objective, such as initial access, persistence,

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.000
privilege escalation, defense evasion, credential access, discovery, lateral movement,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.930
collection, exfiltration, or impact.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.919
A technique describes a way to accomplish that objective.

00:01:07.919 --> 00:01:12.000
Procedures are the specific ways a threat actor or campaign has implemented a technique.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.490
TTPs are often more durable than single indicators because behavior can persist even when

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:17.908
infrastructure changes.

00:01:17.908 --> 00:01:22.155
MITRE ATT&CK is a widely used knowledge base for organizing adversary tactics and

00:01:22.155 --> 00:01:23.000
techniques.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.220
It gives defenders a shared language for mapping observations, detections,

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:28.020
hunt hypotheses, and coverage gaps.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:33.100
ATT&CK is not a complete model of every threat and it should not be used mechanically,

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:36.090
but it is useful for connecting low-level evidence to behavior.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.985
For example, an unusual process, command-line pattern,

00:01:38.985 --> 00:01:42.150
and authentication event may be more meaningful when mapped to a likely technique and

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:43.075
defensive objective.

00:01:43.075 --> 00:01:45.020
The distinction matters during response.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:46.750
If a team only blocks a list of domains,

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:49.985
the adversary or campaign may shift infrastructure and continue.

00:01:49.985 --> 00:01:55.020
If the team also understands the behavior, such as suspicious authentication patterns,

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.105
unusual script execution, or unexpected data staging,

00:01:57.105 --> 00:02:01.390
defenders can build detections and hunts that remain useful after individual indicators

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:02.055
expire.

00:02:02.055 --> 00:02:04.070
Indicators are often fast to use.

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:05.965
Behavior is often more resilient.

00:02:05.965 --> 00:02:10.105
A mature CTI workflow connects indicators to behavior whenever possible.

00:02:10.105 --> 00:02:14.000
Instead of treating every IOC as equally meaningful, ask what the indicator represents,

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.000
how it was observed, how recent it is, what behavior it supports,

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.075
and whether that behavior matters locally.

00:02:19.075 --> 00:02:20.965
Indicators can be tactical starting points.

00:02:20.965 --> 00:02:24.020
TTPs help defenders build more resilient detections, better hunts,

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:26.075
and clearer incident response guidance.

00:02:26.075 --> 00:02:28.070
This does not mean indicators are unimportant.

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:32.100
They are often useful for quick scoping, enrichment, and short-term containment.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.015
The key is to label their purpose.

00:02:34.015 --> 00:02:37.985
An indicator used for enrichment may be acceptable with lower confidence than an indicator

00:02:37.985 --> 00:02:39.085
used for blocking.

00:02:39.085 --> 00:02:41.970
Matching the control to the confidence reduces unnecessary harm.
